# Copy this file, don't edit it in place — .env is gitignored, this one isn't.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#
# Quote every value in single quotes. That second line sources .env as a shell
# script, so an unquoted value gets executed or mangled — and a value that is a
# syntax error (an unquoted `Alerts <a@b.com>`) aborts the load at that line,
# leaving every variable below it unset while still exiting 0. For a literal
# apostrophe inside a value, close-escape-reopen: PASS='it'\''s'
#
# In GitHub Actions these come from repository secrets, not this file.
# evening.yml forwards the six secrets, SCAN_FEED as a repository variable,
# SCAN_SESSION_DATE from the Run-workflow form's `session` box -- a scheduled
# run has no box, so that one arrives as '' and the scanner reads it as unset
# -- and DRY_RUN_FLAG from the same form's `dry_run` box, which is the whole
# env block of its pipeline step and not a list typed from memory.
# One variable in this file it does NOT forward: CLAUDE_MODEL. Three sentences
# here said a pinned session was local-only too, while a fourth below and the
# workflow's own env block said the opposite; a reader backfilling from Actions
# is the one who needed that sentence and the one it misled.
# One variable NEITHER workflow forwards and every runner sets by itself:
# $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY, the file a step writes the markdown block that renders
# on the run's own page into. The pipeline appends the mailed subject, the exit
# code and every problem to it. Unset or empty -- which is every run outside
# Actions, including yours -- means no block is written and nothing else
# changes, so there is nothing to put in this file.
# morning.yml forwards only the three delivery variables, because the
# follow-through pass runs neither the scanner nor the scorer -- which is also
# why its preflight names only RESEND_API_KEY and EMAIL_TO when they are
# missing, and not the five the evening run demands.
# publish-dashboard.yml uses only Actions' built-in GITHUB_TOKEN (pages: write),
# not a key from this file. It requests a Pages build of committed main after
# the evening workflow completes and verifies the public HTML and JSON files.
# A scan artifact is never used as its source, including a dry-run artifact.
# The evening cron skips a published session only when this branch has both
# its matching artifact and a committed, non-fixture universe snapshot.
#
# Note what an EVENING run THAT SCANS writes, since --dry-run does not stop
# it: docs/data.json (rewritten), docs/ledger.json (appended to),
# docs/charts/*.png and results/<session>_evening.csv (gitignored; the
# scored rows, one file per session). A run whose session docs/data.json
# already holds scans nothing and writes none of them -- see
# SCAN_SESSION_DATE below, which is how to ask for the scan anyway.
# docs/data.json is whatever the last run wrote: on a fresh clone of this
# repo that is the run evening.yml last committed back, real since 6 Sep
# 2026, and it is still the hand-authored fixture only in a repo that has
# never published — so a local smoke test replaces it, and adds a run to
# docs/ledger.json that later runs read as history (marked as a --tickers
# run in runs[].universe, but a row all the same). `rm -f docs/ledger.json &&
# git checkout -- docs/` puts both back -- checkout alone does it here,
# since the first commit-back tracked docs/ledger.json along with
# docs/data.json. The rm is kept for a repo that has never published, where
# the ledger is untracked and checkout leaves it.
# See README, "Run locally". The PNGs are gitignored and never committed, so they stay on the machine that ran the
# pipeline; the evening email attaches them, the published dashboard says
# where they went. A MORNING run writes nothing at all: it re-presents the run
# docs/data.json already holds, refuses to present the fixture, and attaches
# no chart.

# --- Required -------------------------------------------------------------
# All five are checked by preflight() BEFORE the scan starts (step 5), so a
# missing one costs nothing. Which five depends on the mode, since step 10:
# `morning` needs only RESEND_API_KEY and EMAIL_TO, because it makes no data
# request and no model call. Demanding the others would refuse a run that
# would have worked. They used to fail in three different ways: ALPACA_*
# aborted deep inside the SDK, RESEND_API_KEY and EMAIL_TO were not read until
# send_email() at the very end — after the whole scan and every paid Claude call
# — and a missing ANTHROPIC_API_KEY did not fail at all, it quietly mailed
# checklist scores dressed as AI ones. Now the run stops before spending
# anything and names every variable that is missing, not just the first.
#
# An empty value counts as missing: in Actions an unset repository secret
# expands to '' rather than being absent, which is not the same thing to
# os.environ but is the same thing to a user.

# Alpaca (https://alpaca.markets -> Home -> API Keys).
# Paper or live keys both work now. get_clients() builds only a
# StockHistoricalDataClient, which has no paper/live distinction; the
# TradingClient that used to pin this to paper was deleted along with the
# asset-list call, since the universe is now a checked-in file. Nothing in this
# pipeline places orders, so paper keys remain the smaller blast radius.
# The scan no longer takes whatever feed the plan defaults to. _download_batch()
# names one — sip, the consolidated tape — because an end-of-day scan has no
# use for real-time data and needs consolidated volume, where a free plan's IEX
# default carries one venue's slice of it. On a plan without a real-time
# subscription a SIP request's window has to end at least fifteen minutes
# behind the clock, and the scan ends its own window sixteen minutes behind
# it when the session's day would run past that (an older session's window
# already ends earlier and is left alone). It asked
# for `delayed_sip` for nine rounds; the first live run (6 Sep 2026) showed the
# bars endpoint does not take that name at all ("invalid feed"), and a feed the
# endpoint or the plan refuses now aborts the run with FeedNotAuthorizedError
# naming it, rather than dropping every batch and reporting an empty market.
# Set SCAN_FEED below to change it.
# Daily volume includes extended-hours trades and can still change after the
# 6:16 PM ET scan. The feed delay is an access limit, not a finality guarantee.
# Neither volume gate is an absolute number any more (step 4). A burst has to
# clear 1.5x the stock's OWN trailing 50-session average, and its dollar volume
# has to sit in the top 70% of the names that traded that session. Both are
# ratios, so a feed reporting a fraction of the consolidated tape shrinks the
# numerator and the denominator together and the shortlist barely moves — where
# the old 5,000,000-share floor rejected almost everything on a free plan's IEX
# feed, and, on any feed, rejected a $180 leader trading 4.9M shares while
# admitting a $4.50 stock trading 6M. Neither threshold is settable from the
# environment; change ScanConfig if you want to retune them.
ALPACA_API_KEY=''
ALPACA_SECRET_KEY=''
# This client's HTTP requests use 5-second connection and 30-second read
# inactivity timeouts, including pagination and forward-return fills. Existing
# rate-limit and batch retries are unchanged. These are scanner constants;
# they add no environment variable and are not a total response deadline.

# Anthropic (https://console.anthropic.com -> API Keys). Needs billing enabled.
# Read implicitly by anthropic.Anthropic() in scorer._client().
# Scoring connects within 5 seconds and has 30-second read/write/pool inactivity
# limits, with no SDK retries. The pipeline makes at most two attempts per
# candidate before a marked fallback; these limits are constants in scorer.py.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=''

# Resend (https://resend.com/api-keys). Not needed for --dry-run.
RESEND_API_KEY=''

# Recipients, comma-separated. Not needed for --dry-run. Until a domain is
# verified at resend.com/domains, Resend delivers only to the address the
# account is registered under, so this has to be exactly that one address,
# alone -- a second recipient or a stale value draws a refusal the run log
# diagnoses; a capital letter is corrected on the spot, since Resend's check
# is an exact string match, and the log says to re-save it in that spelling.
EMAIL_TO='you@example.com'

# --- Optional locally, but set it in CI -----------------------------------

# Sender. send_email() falls back to onboarding@resend.dev, which Resend only
# delivers to the account owner. evening.yml always sets RESEND_FROM, so an
# unset repository secret arrives as '' rather than as absent -- and the
# fallback reads it with .strip() precisely so that BOTH spellings of absent
# reach it. It used to test only for a missing variable, so CI mailed from
# nobody and Resend refused every send. Set a verified-domain sender to mail
# anyone but the account owner.
# RESEND_FROM='alerts@yourdomain.com'

# Scoring model. scorer.py defaults to claude-sonnet-4-6. Local only —
# evening.yml does not forward CLAUDE_MODEL, so CI always runs the default. It
# is the only variable here the workflow does not pass. This said a pinned
# session was the other; evening.yml forwards SCAN_SESSION_DATE from the
# Run-workflow form's `session` box, and has since the first live day.
# CLAUDE_MODEL='claude-sonnet-4-6'

# --- Optional: overriding the data request --------------------------------
# Both are read when ScanConfig() is built, and both raise on a value they do
# not understand rather than falling back to the default — a typo here must
# not quietly change which data the scan ran on.
#
# SCAN_FEED IS forwarded by evening.yml, as a repository VARIABLE (Settings →
# Secrets and variables → Actions → Variables) rather than a secret, because
# it is not a credential. It is the one knob a refused feed needs: the scan
# asks for sip, and an account that may not query it aborts the run with
# FeedNotAuthorizedError naming the feed — setting SCAN_FEED=iex is then the
# fix, and until now it could only be applied by editing the workflow. An
# unset variable arrives as '', which is treated as "use the default".
#
# READ THE ERROR'S NAME BEFORE REACHING FOR THIS. A rejected KEY aborts with
# CredentialsRejectedError instead, and SCAN_FEED is not its fix — check the
# two ALPACA_ secrets. The two used to arrive as one error telling you to
# subscribe, so a typo sent you shopping for a data plan.
# SCAN_SESSION_DATE reaches a run in Actions too, from the Run-workflow form's
# `session` box rather than from a secret: a backfill is deliberate, and the
# first live day needed one from there. A scheduled run has no box and sends
# '', so a nightly cron carries no pin.

# Which Alpaca feed to read: iex, sip, delayed_sip, otc, boats, overnight.
# Defaults to sip. Set 'iex' to go back to the free single-venue feed, which
# needs no hold-back; delayed_sip is a name the bars endpoint refuses.
# SCAN_FEED='iex'

# The session to scan, YYYY-MM-DD. Unset, the scanner derives it: today's
# session once 16:15 ET has passed, otherwise the previous one, never a
# weekend. Holidays are deliberately not in that arithmetic — a run on one
# finds no bar carrying the session it asked for and stops with
# StaleDataError instead of re-reading the last trading day as "today". The
# day AFTER a holiday needs no pin: the session before it is read off the
# frames the scan fetched, since a business day no name printed is a
# closure -- and one name that DID print on it settles the question the
# other way (README, "The session before is read off the frames"). Set this to
# scan a past session, or to force a run on a day the market was closed.
#
# It is also what makes an evening run RE-SCAN a session docs/data.json
# already holds. Unpinned, such a run re-presents that session instead --
# whatever the clock says, so a click made after the nightly cron has
# published is covered as well as a lunchtime one, and so is a rehearsal from
# the form's dry_run box, which then makes no request to the feed at all --
# because a second scan of the same daily bars buys the same answer, pays for
# every Claude call again and replaces the published record. It is consulted
# before the scan, so nothing is fetched on the way to that decision.
#
# The BASKET is part of it: a --tickers record is not the night's universe
# scan published, so the cron that follows a smoke test still scans the file.
# A --tickers run against a published universe scan does re-present, because
# publishing it would replace that night's record with a narrower one.
# Explicit records also keep run.universe.tickers: equal counts with different
# symbols are different scans; reordering the same symbols is not. Older
# records missing that list are preserved with an identity-unknown explanation.
#
# A pin is you asking for the re-scan -- and asking for that overwrite:
# the ledger keys a run on its session and type, so pinning a smoke test to a
# session the record already holds drops that night's rows and its filled
# benchmark. Pin a session the record does not hold, or put both files back
# afterwards (README, "Run locally").
#
# AND IT IS NOT THE FIX FOR EVERY StaleDataError. When no symbol carried a bar
# for the session, the error's own last sentence advised pinning it -- which,
# on a run that had ALREADY pinned it, is advice to repeat what just failed:
# the same request to the same feed answers the same way. Pinned, the error
# now says the feed holds no bar for that session; unpinned, it names the
# possibility the clock cannot see (a day the market held no session) and
# offers a DIFFERENT session, not this one. The failure notice mails whichever
# sentence applies.
#
# The OTHER StaleDataError -- a majority of the names behind the session while
# a minority printed for it -- gets neither of those, because one name that
# printed is the disproof of a closure. It is told a closure cannot explain
# this and named what can: a feed writing part of the tape, a session still
# open for the names that have not printed yet, or a halt across them. Both
# raises used to end on the sentences above, so a partial feed outage was
# mailed as a possible market holiday.
#
# Since step 10 setting this also EXEMPTS the run from the mode/clock check.
# An evening run started before the close, or a morning one after it, is
# normally reported as degraded with the session it really read named in the
# email; a pinned session is you overruling the clock deliberately, and a
# documented backfill must not be reported as a mistake. The pin then stands
# in for "what the clock would have said" throughout the run, so the morning
# mode follows through on the pinned session too. evening.yml forwards it
# from the Run-workflow form's `session` box; a scheduled run has no box and
# sends '', which is read as unset.
#
# Since step 9 this is also how a history is seeded. Forward returns are
# measured through the newest COMPLETED session, not through the session
# scanned, so a run pinned to an old one resolves its own candidates' d1/d3/d5
# immediately instead of waiting a week for them — and each such run appends to
# docs/ledger.json rather than replacing it. One run per past session builds a
# dataset to check the scores against.
# SCAN_SESSION_DATE='2026-08-31'

# The optional private trading service uses a separate deployment environment:
# see deploy/broker/env.example and deploy/broker/README.md.
# Never place OAuth secrets, tokens, account records or the private database
# in docs/ or any public hosting directory. Scanner credentials do not enable
# trading on GitHub Pages.
